Now let's implement rename. Approach: load all notes, find the note with old_id, error
 if it doesn't exist, delete the old file, save with new id and new title. But `save
` writes to `{id}.note`. load_all reads all files in the directory (including non
-.note files, but that's fine).

Simplest and most robust implementation:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io
::Result<()> {
    let mut notes = load_all(dir)?;
    let note = notes.iter_mut().find(|n| n.id == old_id)
        .ok_or_else(|| std::io::Error::new(std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound, format!("
note with id {} not found", old_id)))?;
    note.id = new_id.to_string();
    note.title = new_title.to_string();
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    if old_path.exists() {
        fs::remove_file(&old_path)?;
    }
    save(dir, note)
}
```

Edge case: what if old_id == new_id? In that case we delete and then re-save the file
 — no problem. What if new_id already exists? In that case two files get written,
 and save overwrites the existing new_id file. load_all will return one note for new
_id. The requirement is to leave no trace under old_id — this is satisfied. Overwriting
 an existing note is acceptable behavior.

One caveat: load_all reads all files in the directory, including files that are not
 .note. If the old file is not named `{old_id}.note`, the old trace will remain. But
 save always writes to `{id}.note`, so the file name is derived from the id. No problem
.

